1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,835
This is ECE 358, video 9. The topic is Selective Repeat and the window size of pipelined protocols.

2
00:00:10,285 --> 00:00:33,794
After this video, you can explain the sender and receiver windows of Selective Repeat. You can trace Selective Repeat when a packet is lost. You can find the largest window size for a sequence number space. And you can calculate the sender utilization. At the end, we solve QB questions 30 to 35, and Problem 3 of Problem Set 3.

3
00:00:34,244 --> 00:00:46,447
First, the problem that Selective Repeat solves. This is Go-Back-N with a window of 4, from the last video. The sender sends packets 0 to 3. Packet 2 is lost.

4
00:00:46,897 --> 00:00:58,737
Packets 0 and 1 arrive. The receiver delivers them and sends ack 0 and ack 1. Each ACK moves the sender window, so the sender sends packets 4 and 5.

5
00:00:59,187 --> 00:01:11,112
Packets 3, 4 and 5 arrive out of order. The Go-Back-N receiver discards them. It sends ack 1 again each time. The sender ignores these duplicate ACKs.

6
00:01:11,562 --> 00:01:25,258
The timer of packet 2 expires. The sender sends packets 2, 3, 4 and 5 again. So one lost packet costs 4 transmissions. With a large window, this wastes much of the link.

7
00:01:25,708 --> 00:01:39,191
Selective Repeat changes both ends. The sender sends packets 0 to 5. Packet 2 is lost. Packets 0 and 1 arrive in order. The receiver delivers them and ACKs each one.

8
00:01:39,641 --> 00:01:55,662
Packets 3, 4 and 5 arrive after a gap. The Selective Repeat receiver does not discard them. It buffers them and ACKs each one individually. A packet is correctly received when its sequence number is in the receive window.

9
00:01:56,112 --> 00:02:04,624
The sender keeps one timer for each packet. Only the timer of packet 2 expires. So the sender resends only packet 2.

10
00:02:05,074 --> 00:02:15,186
Packet 2 fills the gap. Now the receiver delivers 2, 3, 4 and 5 in order to the upper layer. One loss costs one retransmission.

11
00:02:15,636 --> 00:02:27,711
Here is the Selective Repeat sender window from slide 39. Each box is one sequence number, from 0 to 23. The colour gives the state of each sequence number.

12
00:02:28,161 --> 00:02:39,979
The window has N equal to 12 consecutive sequence numbers, from 7 to 18. send base points to the oldest packet that is not ACKed. Here it is 7.

13
00:02:40,429 --> 00:02:52,355
The green boxes, 9, 10, 12 and 13, are already ACKed. Their ACKs arrived out of order. In Selective Repeat, the sender records each ACK on its own.

14
00:02:52,805 --> 00:03:01,509
The orange boxes, 7, 8, 11 and 14, are sent but not yet ACKed. Each of them has its own timer.

15
00:03:01,959 --> 00:03:13,308
The blue boxes, 15 to 18, are in the window but not yet used. next seq num points to 15. The white boxes outside the window are not usable.

16
00:03:13,758 --> 00:03:19,369
Now ACKs arrive. This is the start state again. The window is 7 to 18.

17
00:03:19,819 --> 00:03:35,136
ACK 7 arrives. The sender marks 7 as ACKed. 7 is equal to send base. So send base moves to the next unACKed sequence number, which is 8. The window becomes 8 to 19.

18
00:03:35,586 --> 00:03:53,762
Then ACK 8 arrives. 8 is equal to send base. The next unACKed numbers after 8 are not 9 or 10, because those are already ACKed. So send base jumps to 11. The window becomes 11 to 22, as on the slide.

19
00:03:54,212 --> 00:04:03,471
Now 15 to 22 are usable. That is 8 new sequence numbers. The window moves only when the oldest packet is ACKed.

20
00:04:03,921 --> 00:04:06,481
Slide 40 starts from the same state.

21
00:04:06,931 --> 00:04:19,155
The timer of packet 7 expires. The sender resends packet 7 only. It restarts the timer of packet 7. Packets 8, 11 and 14 are not sent again.

22
00:04:19,605 --> 00:04:35,541
Then 2 new packets arrive from the upper layer. The next sequence number, 15, is in the window. So the sender sends them as 15 and 16. Now next seq num is 17. The slide's drawing keeps the pointer at 15.

23
00:04:35,991 --> 00:04:42,263
A timeout does not move the window. The sender only resends the one packet that timed out.

24
00:04:42,713 --> 00:04:59,395
Slide 46 lists the rules of the sender. There are three events. This example uses a window of 4. First, data comes from the upper layer. If the next sequence number is in the window, the sender sends the packet and starts its timer.

25
00:04:59,845 --> 00:05:08,229
Second, the timer of packet n expires. The sender resends packet n only, and restarts its timer. Here n is 1.

26
00:05:08,679 --> 00:05:26,834
Third, ACK n arrives. The sender marks packet n as ACKed. If n is equal to send base, send base moves to the next unACKed sequence number. Here ACK 0 arrives and 1 is already ACKed, so send base moves to 2.

27
00:05:27,284 --> 00:05:30,953
The sender uses ACK n only if n is in its window.

28
00:05:31,403 --> 00:05:44,331
The receiver also keeps a window of N equal to 12. Here packets 8, 9 and 11 arrived out of order. The receiver buffered them and ACKed each one. They are green.

29
00:05:44,781 --> 00:05:52,163
Packets 7 and 10 are expected but not yet received. They are orange. They block the in-order delivery.

30
00:05:52,613 --> 00:06:06,479
The window starts at receive base. receive base is the smallest sequence number that is expected and not yet received. Here it is 7. The window is 7 to 18. The blue numbers are acceptable.

31
00:06:06,929 --> 00:06:23,164
Now packet 7 arrives. It is equal to receive base. The receiver delivers 7, then the buffered 8 and 9, in order. Packet 10 is still missing. So receive base moves to 10, and the window becomes 10 to 21.

32
00:06:23,614 --> 00:06:33,726
If packet 10 arrives next, the receiver delivers 10 and the buffered 11. Then receive base is 12, and the window is 12 to 23.

33
00:06:34,176 --> 00:06:57,557
Slide 46 also lists the rules of the receiver. Packet n arrives. In this example, receive base is 10 and N is 6. First case: n is in the window, 10 to 15. The receiver sends ACK n. It buffers an out-of-order packet. For an in-order packet, it delivers it with the buffered packets after it, and moves the window.

34
00:06:58,007 --> 00:07:06,711
Second case: n is in the previous window, 4 to 9. The receiver already delivered this packet. It sends ACK n again.

35
00:07:07,161 --> 00:07:11,748
Third case: any other sequence number. The receiver ignores the packet.

36
00:07:12,198 --> 00:07:25,275
Why is the second case necessary? Here packet 9 arrives and the receiver delivers it. But ack 9 is lost. The timer of packet 9 expires, and the sender resends packet 9.

37
00:07:25,725 --> 00:07:37,437
9 is now in the previous window. The receiver does not deliver it again, but it sends ack 9 again. Without this ACK, the sender window never moves past 9.

38
00:07:37,887 --> 00:07:48,362
Here is Selective Repeat in action, with the same loss as before. The window is 4. The sender sends packets 0 to 3. Packet 2 is lost.

39
00:07:48,812 --> 00:07:59,948
Packets 0 and 1 arrive. The receiver delivers them and sends ack 0 and ack 1. The sender window moves, and the sender sends packets 4 and 5.

40
00:08:00,398 --> 00:08:13,689
Packets 3, 4 and 5 arrive out of order. The receiver buffers each one and sends ack 3, ack 4 and ack 5. The sender records each of these ACKs. send base stays at 2.

41
00:08:14,139 --> 00:08:20,581
The timer of packet 2 expires. The sender resends packet 2, but not 3, 4 or 5.

42
00:08:21,031 --> 00:08:28,562
Packet 2 arrives. The receiver delivers packets 2, 3, 4 and 5 in order, and sends ack 2.

43
00:08:29,012 --> 00:08:46,441
The slide asks: what happens when ack 2 arrives? Ack 2 ACKs send base. Packets 3, 4 and 5 are already ACKed. So send base moves to 6, and the window becomes 6 to 9. One loss cost only one retransmission.

44
00:08:46,891 --> 00:09:02,443
Before the dilemma, we need one fact. The sequence number is a field of k bits in the packet header. So it has 2 to the power k values, from 0 to 2 to the power k, minus 1. This set is the sequence number space.

45
00:09:02,893 --> 00:09:08,248
For example, k equal to 2 gives 4 values: 0, 1, 2 and 3.

46
00:09:08,698 --> 00:09:17,359
After 3, the numbers start again at 0. Packet 4 gets sequence number 0, packet 5 gets 1, and so on.

47
00:09:17,809 --> 00:09:27,985
So packets 0, 4 and 8 all carry sequence number 0. The receiver sees only the number. It cannot tell which of these packets arrived.

48
00:09:28,435 --> 00:09:45,929
Slide 48 shows a dilemma. The sequence numbers are 0, 1, 2 and 3. The window size is 3. The sender sends packets 0, 1 and 2. After each one, the receiver window moves. Now the receiver accepts 3, 0 and 1.

49
00:09:46,379 --> 00:09:57,856
In case a, ACK 0 and ACK 1 arrive. The sender window moves. The sender sends packet 3, which is lost, and then a new packet with sequence number 0.

50
00:09:58,306 --> 00:10:05,453
The receiver accepts sequence number 0. It is the new packet 0. This is correct. No problem.

51
00:10:05,903 --> 00:10:16,036
Case b starts the same way. The sender sends packets 0, 1 and 2. The receiver accepts them, and its window becomes 3, 0, 1.

52
00:10:16,486 --> 00:10:18,811
But now all three ACKs are lost.

53
00:10:19,261 --> 00:10:25,107
The timer of packet 0 expires. The sender retransmits the old packet 0.

54
00:10:25,557 --> 00:10:36,543
The receiver window contains 0. So the receiver accepts this old copy as new data. The data of packet 0 is delivered twice. This is a problem.

55
00:10:36,993 --> 00:10:45,612
In both cases, the receiver is in the same state and sees the same sequence number 0. It cannot know which case it is in.

56
00:10:46,062 --> 00:11:02,723
Why does this happen? At one time, the sender can still resend its oldest N sequence numbers. If all ACKs are lost, the receiver already accepts the next N sequence numbers. With a window of 3, that is 6 numbers in a space of only 4.

57
00:11:03,173 --> 00:11:11,493
So the numbers 0 and 1 are in both groups. A packet with sequence number 0 can be an old copy or new data.

58
00:11:11,943 --> 00:11:23,442
With a window of 2, the two groups use 4 numbers. Each number has one meaning. The receiver accepts only 2 and 3, so the old packet 0 is not accepted.

59
00:11:23,892 --> 00:11:34,964
So the window size must be less than or equal to half of the sequence number space. The slide's answer: reduce the window size to 2, which is half of 4.

60
00:11:35,414 --> 00:11:47,745
Now, the performance of a sliding window when there are no errors. The sender sends the first bit at time 0. The last bit leaves at time L over R, the transmission time.

61
00:11:48,195 --> 00:11:59,715
With a window of W packets, the sender sends W packets back to back. Here W is 3. So the sender is busy for W times L over R.

62
00:12:00,165 --> 00:12:11,194
The first bit arrives one propagation delay later. When the last bit of a packet arrives, the receiver sends an ACK. Here, the ACK has no transmission time.

63
00:12:11,644 --> 00:12:26,471
The first ACK arrives at the sender at time RTT plus L over R. Before that, the window is full and the sender is idle. This time from 0 to RTT plus L over R is one cycle.

64
00:12:26,921 --> 00:12:33,577
When the ACK arrives, the window moves and the sender sends the next packet. The same cycle repeats.

65
00:12:34,027 --> 00:12:53,696
The utilization of the sender is the fraction of time that it sends. In one cycle, it is busy for W times L over R. The cycle is RTT plus L over R. So U is W times L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

66
00:12:54,146 --> 00:13:13,538
But the sender cannot be busy more than all the time. So U is the minimum of 1 and this ratio. On the plot, U grows in a straight line with W, and then stays at 1. This example uses L over R of 1 ms and an RTT of 9 ms.

67
00:13:13,988 --> 00:13:36,004
The ideal window size makes U exactly 1. Set W times L over R equal to RTT plus L over R. Then W upper bound is RTT plus L over R, divided by L over R. Here, 10 divided by 1 gives 10 packets. A larger window does not help.

68
00:13:36,454 --> 00:13:43,366
For W equal to 3, U is 3 times 1, divided by 10. That is 0 point 3.

69
00:13:43,816 --> 00:14:00,115
W equal to 1 is stop-and-wait. U is 1 divided by 10, which is 0 point 1. The old slide 50 writes the same formula with t T for RTT plus L over R, and t I for L over R.

70
00:14:00,565 --> 00:14:09,525
Now the question bank. Question 30 asks for a table that compares Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat. Pause the video and try it.

71
00:14:09,975 --> 00:14:21,409
The given row is timers. Go-Back-N has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. Selective Repeat has one timer for each unACKed packet.

72
00:14:21,859 --> 00:14:26,531
Both protocols can have up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline.

73
00:14:26,981 --> 00:14:36,133
Go-Back-N uses cumulative ACKs. ACK n covers all packets up to n. Selective Repeat ACKs each packet individually.

74
00:14:36,583 --> 00:14:47,826
When an out-of-order packet arrives, the Go-Back-N receiver discards it and re-ACKs the highest in-order sequence number. The Selective Repeat receiver buffers it and ACKs it.

75
00:14:48,276 --> 00:15:02,057
When a timer expires, Go-Back-N resends all unACKed packets. Selective Repeat resends only that packet. With one loss and N equal to 4, that is 4 packets against 1.

76
00:15:02,507 --> 00:15:11,809
The Go-Back-N receiver expects one sequence number only. The Selective Repeat receiver has a window of N, and delivers in order.

77
00:15:12,259 --> 00:15:19,021
Selective Repeat also has a limit: the window size is at most half of the sequence number space.

78
00:15:19,471 --> 00:15:25,402
Questions 31 to 33 are short calculations. Pause the video and try them.

79
00:15:25,852 --> 00:15:38,332
Question 31. A field of 8 bits has 2 to the power 8 values. So the sequence number space has 256 numbers, from 0 to 255.

80
00:15:38,782 --> 00:15:47,251
Question 32. Work backwards. 2048 is 2 to the power 11. So the field needs 11 bits.

81
00:15:47,701 --> 00:16:02,656
Question 33. The window size must be at most half of the sequence number space. 4096 divided by 2 is 2048. So the maximum window size is 2048.

82
00:16:03,106 --> 00:16:09,442
Question 34 asks you to derive the ideal window size. Pause the video and try it.

83
00:16:09,892 --> 00:16:17,188
The sender sends the first packet at time 0. Its ACK returns at time RTT plus L over R.

84
00:16:17,638 --> 00:16:27,345
Until that ACK arrives, the sender can only send the packets of its window. W packets keep it busy for W times L over R.

85
00:16:27,795 --> 00:16:36,627
The sender is never idle if W times L over R is greater than or equal to RTT plus L over R.

86
00:16:37,077 --> 00:16:59,519
So the max useful window is RTT plus L over R, divided by L over R. That is 1 plus RTT divided by L over R. In the picture, L over R is 1 and RTT is 9. 10 packets fill the cycle exactly, and the first ACK arrives when packet 10 ends.

87
00:16:59,969 --> 00:17:06,881
Question 35 asks for the utilization with a given window W. Pause the video and try it.

88
00:17:07,331 --> 00:17:20,857
If W is smaller than the upper bound, the sender is busy for W times L over R in each cycle of RTT plus L over R. So U is the ratio of these two times.

89
00:17:21,307 --> 00:17:28,261
If W is equal to or larger than the upper bound, the sender is never idle, and U is 1.

90
00:17:28,711 --> 00:17:42,322
Both cases together give one expression: U equals the minimum of 1, and W times L over R divided by RTT plus L over R. This is note 2 of slide 49.

91
00:17:42,772 --> 00:18:05,620
Now Problem 3 of Problem Set 3. Node A sends frames to node C through node B. Link A B is 2000 miles long and runs at 300 kbps, with a sliding window of 5. Link B C is 500 miles long and uses stop-and-wait. Part a asks for the minimum rate of link B C.

92
00:18:06,070 --> 00:18:27,681
All data frames are 1500 bits. The ACKs have negligible length. The propagation delay is 12 microseconds per mile on both lines. The lines are full duplex, and there are no errors. Part b swaps the protocols: stop-and-wait on A B, and a window of 5 on B C.

93
00:18:28,131 --> 00:18:39,885
The buffers of B do not flood if frames leave B at least as fast as they arrive. So the throughput of B C must be at least the throughput of A B. Pause the video and try it.

94
00:18:40,335 --> 00:18:59,898
Part a, link A B first. The transmission time of one frame is 1500 bits divided by 300 kbps, which is 5 ms. The propagation delay is 12 microseconds times 2000 miles, which is 24 ms.

95
00:19:00,348 --> 00:19:12,039
The first ACK comes back after d plus 2 P. That is 5 plus 48, which is 53 ms. This is the cycle, RTT plus L over R.

96
00:19:12,489 --> 00:19:29,342
With a window of 5, the sender is busy for 5 times 5, which is 25 ms of each 53 ms cycle. So the efficiency is 25 divided by 53, which is 0 point 4 7 1 7.

97
00:19:29,792 --> 00:19:50,571
The throughput is the efficiency times the rate. 0 point 4 7 1 7 times 300 kbps is 141 point 5 kbps. Divided by 1500 bits, that is 94 point 3 4 frames per second into node B.

98
00:19:51,021 --> 00:20:07,959
Now link B C, with an unknown rate x. The propagation delay is 12 microseconds times 500 miles, which is 6 ms. One cycle is 1500 over x, plus 2 times 6 ms.

99
00:20:08,409 --> 00:20:17,519
Stop-and-wait sends one frame per cycle. So the throughput is 1 over T frames per second. The green curve shows it against x.

100
00:20:17,969 --> 00:20:37,702
A faster link makes 1500 over x small, but the 12 ms of propagation stays. So even with an infinite rate, the throughput is 1 over 12 ms. That is 83 point 3 frames per second, or 125 kbps.

101
00:20:38,152 --> 00:20:53,789
Node B receives 94 point 3 4 frames per second from A. 83 point 3 is less than that. So no rate on link B C is enough, and the buffers of B flood. This is the answer of the solution.

102
00:20:54,239 --> 00:21:12,330
Part b. Now link A B uses stop-and-wait. One frame per cycle of 53 ms gives 18 point 8 7 frames per second. Times 1500 bits, that is 28 point 3 kbps into node B.

103
00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:37,441
Link B C now has a window of 5. Its efficiency is the minimum of 1, and 5 times 1500 over x, divided by 1500 over x plus 12 ms. Multiply by x on top and bottom: 7500 divided by 0 point 0 1 2 x plus 1500.

104
00:21:37,891 --> 00:21:48,281
This ratio is 1 or more for every rate up to 500 kbps. So for these rates, the link is never idle, and its throughput is x.

105
00:21:48,731 --> 00:22:02,683
The solution first assumes that the efficiency is less than 1. Then it sets the throughput equal to 28 point 3 kbps, and finds x equal to 5 point 9 3 kbps.

106
00:22:03,133 --> 00:22:13,714
Then it checks the assumption. At 5 point 9 3 kbps, the ratio is 4 point 8. That is more than 1, so the assumption is false.

107
00:22:14,164 --> 00:22:25,983
So the efficiency is 1, and the throughput of B C is x. The rate of link B C must be 28 point 3 kbps or more. This matches the solution.

108
00:22:26,433 --> 00:22:49,707
To recap. The Selective Repeat receiver ACKs each packet in its window individually, buffers out-of-order packets, and delivers in order. The sender keeps one timer per packet and resends only the packet that timed out. ACK n moves send base only when n is equal to send base. A packet from the previous window gets a new ACK.

109
00:22:50,157 --> 00:23:06,093
The window size must be at most half of the sequence number space. The utilization is the minimum of 1 and W times L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R. The next video is about TCP.
